= Curve25519 index calculus: an atlas of coordinates,
summation-polynomial symmetry and factor bases
<curve25519-index-calculus-an-atlas-of-coordinates-summation-polynomial-symmetry-and-factor-bases>
Status 2026-11-04. Written for the owner’s question: which coordinates,
point representations, equations and factor bases can speed up index
calculus on Curve25519, and what each one measured.

#strong[Class:] a synthesis of measured studies plus the theory that
bounds them. Every number has a source path. Nothing here is a
leaderboard row. Rho remains ahead on every track, and the distance
remaining is stated in each table.

== 0. How to read this
<how-to-read-this>
- #strong[Curves.] The Curve25519 family miniatures built by
  Curve25519’s own rule (`curve25519_family family`): p the largest
  prime \< 2^k with p ≡ 5 (mod 8), smallest A ≡ 2 (mod 4) with \#E \=
  8r, r prime, twist order 4r′. k \= 20: p \= 1,048,573, A \= 926, r \=
  131,203, √r \= 362.2. Also k \= 16 (A \= 1058), 18 (78), 22 (43186),
  24 (318).
- #strong[Units.] Counted only. GAE \= group additions equivalent. S \=
  (counted GAE + w × unpriced native operations)/√r, priced at w \= 0,
  0.1 and 1 GAE per native operation (AGENTS.md §5a). F4 work is counted
  in multiply-adds and is native (unpriced at w \= 0). No wall-clock
  claim appears anywhere in this atlas.
- #strong[Two tracks.]
  - #emph[Generic track]: a cofactor-projected factor base and a pair
    table (`prime-projected-abscissa` + `mitm`). This is the lab’s
    baseline, `proj-a`.
  - #emph[Algebraic track]: a factor base with an algebraic description
    (an x-interval, a tower) and Semaev’s summation polynomial solved by
    F4 (`pkm-semaev`). Coordinates and symmetries only matter here.
- #strong[Cost model] (validated in §6): F4 work per solve E \= columns
  × r × mpt / covered, where columns \= base size, covered \= exact
  number of subgroup points that are a sum of two base points, and mpt
  \= F4 multiply-adds per target, which depends on (form, t, G) and not
  on the seed or the curve.

== 1. The family’s torsion, and what it rules in and out
<the-familys-torsion-and-what-it-rules-in-and-out>
E(F\_p) ≅ Z/8 × Z/r is cyclic for every family curve (the family rule
requires it). Consequences:

