#strong[The census result that settles the model question.] On five
family curves, over 2,000 random translation frames and 2,000 random
Möbius frames per (curve, t), Weierstrass lo \= 0 and Edwards y are
ordinary draws: 49 of 50 cells inside the central 98% (P1), translation
≡ Möbius with mean ratio 0.986–1.012 (P2)
(`curve25519_frame_search_20261103`). Edwards "beat" Weierstrass in the
earlier study only because lo \= 0 was a below-average draw on that
curve.

#strong[What does work: choosing the frame.] The F4 system is identical
in every frame. Choosing lo per (curve, t) by the log-free coset count J
(read from \[r\]P) raises coverage 2.5–2.8× (t \= 5), about 2× (t \= 6),
1.5–1.7× (t \= 7) over the mean frame, at unchanged per-target cost; in
the engine the F4 work per relation fell 4.71× (t \= 5) and 3.33× (t \=
6) against lo \= 0 at k \= 20.

== 3. Summation polynomials and their symmetry
<summation-polynomials-and-their-symmetry>
=== 3.1 The forms in use
<the-forms-in-use>
- Weierstrass S₃(x₁, x₂, x₃) for y² \= x³ + ax + b, as a quadratic in
  x₃: (x₁ − x₂)² x₃² − 2\[(x₁ + x₂)(x₁x₂ + a) + 2b\] x₃ + (x₁x₂ − a)² −
  4b(x₁ + x₂) (`pkm_semaev.rs`, `s3_in_u`). S₄ is the resultant of two
  S₃’s.
- Montgomery form in u: the same polynomial after x \= u + A/3; its
  coefficients involve A only. Over F\_p coefficient size is irrelevant
  to F4, and the term structure is the same.
- Edwards y form: S₃ with x\_i \= (1 + s) + (1 − s) y\_i over 1 − y\_i,
  denominators cleared (`Membership::EdwardsBits`). Same degree.
- T₀-quotient form: the ordinary S₃ of E/⟨T₀⟩ at w\_i + s equals S₃(z₁,
  z₂, z₃) · S₃(d/z₁, z₂, z₃) up to a monomial (unit tests
  `t0_symmetrised_polynomial_is_s3_times_its_t0_image`, and the baseline
  identity S₃(1/z₁, 1/z₂, z₃) \= S₃/(z₁z₂)²). It vanishes exactly when
  ±P₁ ± P₂ ± P₃ ∈ {O, T₀}.

=== 3.2 The symmetry group of the relation on this family
<the-symmetry-group-of-the-relation-on-this-family>
A symmetry of E acts on the x-line iff it normalises \[−1\], i.e. is a
translation by 2-torsion or an automorphism. On the relation P₁ + ⋯ +
P\_{m+1} \= O:

#align(center)[#table(
  columns: 4,
  align: (col, row) => (left,left,left,left,).at(col),
  inset: 6pt,
  [symmetry], [available here?], [scope], [effect on S\_{m+1}],
  [S\_m permutations of the summands],
  [always],
  [global],
  [S\_{m+1} is symmetric in x₁…x\_m; writing it in elementary symmetric
  functions divides the Gröbner work by about m! only for m ≥ 3],
  [negation],
  [always],
  [per point],
  [already quotiented out by using x],
  [translation by T₀ ∈ E#link("F_p")[2]],
  [yes (one point)],
  [pairwise: applied to an even number of summands],
  [the invariant ring halves the degree in the invariant variable
  (FGHR); on E/⟨T₀⟩ this is the quotient’s own S₃],
  [translation by the other 2-torsion points],
  [no (irrational)],
  [—],
  [—],
  [translation by 4- or 8-torsion],
  [no action on the x-line],
  [—],
  [per-point coordinates cannot see it (§1)],
  [Aut(E)/±1],
  [trivial (j ≠ 0, 1728)],
  [—],
  [no orbit collapse],
  [Frobenius],
  [none (prime field)],
  [—],
  [no Weil restriction],
)
]

So the group acting is S\_m × Z/2, and it is the smallest case in the
literature. What it buys, measured:

- At m \= 2, S₃ has degree 2 in each variable, and symmetrisation cannot
  lower it. Measured: the T₀ form has the same solving degree, the same
  terms and the same F4 cost per target as the plain form in every pair
  (`curve25519_t0_algebraic_20261102`). Its only effect is on the base:
  closing the base under u ↦ 1/u doubles the sums reached (R and R +
  T₀), but at equal column count the closed base has fewer distinct
  sums, so per relation it costs 1.2–3.0× more.
- At m \= 3, S₄ has degree 4 per variable and symmetrisation would
  matter more, but m \= 3 is 2,297× (t \= 4) and 6,643× (t \= 5)
  costlier per relation than selected m \= 2 at k \= 20 (T3). A factor
  of a few from symmetry does not close that.
- The pairwise T₀ action on the #emph[target] side (accept relations for
  R + K, K ∈ E\[8\], the `lifts` oracle) is the generic-track projection
  in disguise: on the generic track projection recovers all 8 cosets and
  beats T₀ keying by 3.3–4.0×; on the algebraic track, after frame or
  seed selection, lifts add only 1.00–1.12× (T1).

=== 3.3 Why the symmetry story is short on this family
<why-the-symmetry-story-is-short-on-this-family>
FGHR’s degree reduction comes from E\[2\] ≅ Z/2 × Z/2 (or from Frobenius
over F\_{p^n}), and the big index-calculus gains in the literature come
from m ≥ 3 with those symmetries. Curve25519’s rational 2-torsion is Z/2
and its field is prime, so the only symmetric reduction available is the
T₀ one, and at m \= 2 it reduces nothing. Everything the algebraic track
gained here came from #emph[selection] (frames, seeds, G), not from
symmetry.

