== 5. Cosets: the mechanism behind every algebraic-track gain
<cosets-the-mechanism-behind-every-algebraic-track-gain>
Write c(P) ∈ Z/8 for the cofactor coset of a base point, read off \[r\]P
without any discrete log. A pair P\_i ± P\_j is a usable relation for a
subgroup target iff c\_i ± c\_j ≡ 0 (mod 8).

- For a signed pair of uniformly random cosets, P(c\_a ± c\_b ≡ 0) \=
  14/64 ≈ 0.22. The census counts something different: covered subgroup
  points (±R counted separately) over unordered column pairs (n(n+1)/2).
  On that denominator a uniform base gives about 0.50 (seed 0: 172/325
  \= 0.53, seed 1093: 528/1081 \= 0.49).
- Selection pushes this up. For seed 1308 at t \= 6, 498 covered points
  over 36·37/2 \= 666 column pairs: 0.75, that is 1.5× the uniform value
  on the same denominator (validator E4).
- The selectors used: J \= number of cancelling pairs (frames, seeds);
  J/columns (T9) because end-to-end cost ∝ columns × r / coverage.
  J/cols picked the best seed of all 2,000 in 14 of 15 (k, t) cells.
- Filtering a base to a single coset class cannot help: keeping only
  coset 4 (or only 0) keeps about 1/8 of the columns and makes every
  pair cancel (at most 2× the selected base’s 0.75 on this denominator),
  which loses to keeping 8× the columns (cost ∝ 1/(columns ×
  p\_cancel)).
- Restricting cosets #emph[algebraically] (2-descent, T₀ quotient)
  raises the polynomial degree and loses; #emph[selecting] among
  parameterisations with an unchanged system wins. That is the lesson of
  waves 1–3.
- Torsion-shifted targets (decompose R + T\_c, c chosen from the coset
  histogram) add 1.00–1.12× after selection (T1 failed its 1.10× rule in
  19 of 20 cells).

