== 7. The grid limit, stated plainly
<the-grid-limit-stated-plainly>
The winner (t \= 5, G \= 4) solves 16 tiny F4 systems per target, 52,816
multiply-adds in total. The exhaustive engine on the same base evaluates
S₃ on every pair of the 32 x-values of V: about n² + 7n \= 1,248 F\_p
multiplications per target from the grid code. So the best F4 arm is
still roughly 40× above brute force on its own factor base, and the
generic pair table (`proj-a`) is 7.4·10³× ahead of it at w \= 0.1. The
joint (t, G, seed) optimisation has moved the F4 track #emph[toward] the
grid limit, not past it. Lead L13 (wave 4) measures this end-to-end with
`engine=exhaustive` and `mitm` on the winner’s base. Until the algebraic
track beats its own grid, its value is in how it scales, not in its
constant.

== 8. Scaling
<scaling>
At fixed (t, G), mpt does not depend on k (T4-rep, T6, T10), and the
#emph[distribution] of columns and covered over seeds at fixed t does
not either (the selected values vary by curve, 28/198 … 39/364 at t \=
6), so E \= columns × r × mpt / covered is linear in r up to that
spread, against rho’s √r. The algebraic track at m \= 2 can only beat
exponent 1 if the optimal t grows with k and mpt(t, G) grows slower than
coverage. At k \= 20 the optimal t is 5 and mpt jumps 13× from (5, 4) to
(6, 4). Lead L10 (wave 4) measures the optimum at k \= 16, 18, 22, 24
and fits the exponent. No 255-bit statement is made or implied.

